and require longer audit retention for their relevant activity.

The key is:

Don't retain everything just because finance exists.

Identify the specific compliance requirement first.

🥉 REAL-TIME POLICY PATTERN #3
🛡️ Security Investigation

Suppose the security team requires:

"Certain security-related audit records must remain available for long-term investigations."

You could design a policy around:

Specific record types / activities → longer duration

This is much more precise than:

"Everything = 10 years."

🟦 REAL-TIME POLICY PATTERN #4
☁️ Critical Workload

Suppose the organization's most sensitive collaboration platform is SharePoint.

The business requirement could be:

"Retain relevant SharePoint audit history longer because it contains critical intellectual property."

Then you design the policy around the appropriate workload/activity scope.

🟣 REAL-TIME POLICY PATTERN #5
👑 Executive / High-Risk Users

Some organizations may have additional monitoring/retention requirements for:

Executives
Administrators
Security teams
Privileged accounts

But again:

⚠️ This should be driven by governance/security requirements, not simply "executives get longer retention."

